let's go a little bit deeper
so we have a model which is what we want to serve so we call it target model M_t 
now we use a draft model M_d to make inference fast. now in each decoding cycle the draft model will propose gamma tokens these will be verified in parallel by M_t.
avg. per token latency 
                            L = (T_draft + T_verify) / tau
                            here T_draft -> time spent generating draft tokens 
                            T_verify -> cost of verification 
                            tau is the expected no. of accepted tokens per cycle
let's say L_target denote the autoregressive per-token latency of M_t
the resulting speedup is nita(n) = L_target / L 

there are basically two types of drafters 
1. Autoregressive drafter this basically generates tokens in seuence this is quite slow 
2. diffusion drafter this generates tokens in parallel within a single forward pass

now let me explain step by step how dflash works
there is basically 2 parts 
1. Inference
so we can't just put a draft model it will give poor acceptence rate. so you train 5-layer block diffusion draft model without any conditioning from target mdoel. 
so here is what happens 
first the input prompt the target model will generate it's first token by performing a standard prefill pass. 
we extract hidden representations from a fixed set of layers unifromly sampled from shallow to deep. we concate teh hidden stats then pass them through a lightweight projection layer fuse cross-layer information into a compact `target context features`.
Now we put these `target context features` into a Key value projection of each draft layer.
a draft layer contains kv cache for tcf a bidirectional attn. a MLP 
all draft layers work in parallel which is the major advantage 
2. Training
KV injections we already discussed 
DFLASH uses random sampling of masked blocks 
for efficient long context training by fixing the number of maksed blocks per sequence and randomly sampling anchor positions for each seq. at every epoch 
we have one problem that In speculative decoding, not all tokens are equal. Errors at early positions within a draft block invalidate all subsequent tokens. we can reflect this by weighting the cross-entropy loss to emphasize earlier token positions during training 
at the end we also have a shared embedding which is token embedding layre and a LM head with the target model 

